</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Hong Kong
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">April 2, 2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Dream 7B, the most powerful open diffusion large language model to date. Unlike autoregressive (AR) models that generate tokens sequentially, Dream 7B employs discrete diffusion modeling to refine sequences in parallel through iterative denoising. Our model consistently outperforms existing diffusion language models on general, mathematical, and coding tasks. Dream 7B demonstrates superior planning abilities and inference flexibility, including arbitrary-order generation, infilling capabilities, and tunable quality-speed trade-offs.
These results are achieved through simple yet effective training techniques, including AR-based LLM initialization and context-adaptive token-level noise rescheduling.
We release both Dream-Base and Dream-Instruct to facilitate further research in diffusion-based language modeling.</p>
  
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2508.15487v1/overall_performance.svg" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/157;" width="476" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Performance comparison of diffusion and autoregressive language models on various tasks.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has fundamentally transformed artificial intelligence, driving breakthroughs across diverse applications and industries. The current landscape is dominated by autoregressive (AR) models that generate text through sequential left-to-right token prediction, with virtually all leading systems including GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and DeepSeek R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
While these models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities, a fundamental question emerges: What architectural paradigms might define the next generation of LLMs? This question becomes increasingly important as AR models exhibit inherent limitations in complex reasoning, long-term planning, and maintaining coherence across extended contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bubeck et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dziri et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bachmann and Nagarajan, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
These limitations are particularly crucial for emerging applications such as embodied AI, autonomous agents, and long-horizon decision-making systems, where the ability to reason holistically about entire sequences rather than incrementally becomes paramount.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Discrete diffusion models (DMs) have gained attention as a promising alternative for sequence generation since their introduction to the text domain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Rather than building text token by token from left to right, diffusion models begin with completely corrupted sequences and gradually denoise them through multiple refinement steps, enabling bidirectional context integration and more flexible generation patterns.
This architectural shift unlocks several theoretical advantages that directly address autoregressive limitations. The parallel nature of diffusion enables richer contextual modeling by incorporating information from all positions simultaneously, potentially leading to more coherent and globally consistent outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, the iterative refinement process naturally supports controllable generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulrajani and Hashimoto, 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and provides flexible quality-speed trade-offs through adjustable inference steps, offering new dimensions for optimization for test-time scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Snell et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geiping et al., 2025</a>)</cite> that complement existing techniques like long-thought chain-of-thought reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaech et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent developments have begun to demonstrate the practical viability of scaled diffusion language models. Works such as DiffuLLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, LLaDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> have successfully scaled discrete diffusion to billion-parameter regimes. While Mercury Coder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inception Labs, 2025</a>)</cite>, as a commercial implementation, has demonstrated remarkable inference efficiency in code generation. This rapid progress, combined with the inherent architectural advantages of diffusion language modeling, positions these models as a promising direction for overcoming the fundamental limitations of autoregressive approaches. However, significant gaps remain in achieving performance parity with state-of-the-art autoregressive models like Qwen2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> across various general tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we present Dream 7B, a 7-billion parameter diffusion language model that bridges this performance gap while demonstrating unique capabilities inherent to the diffusion paradigm.
We introduce a comprehensive training framework that leverages AR-based LLM initialization and context-adaptive noise scheduling to scale diffusion language models. Through extensive evaluation, we establish that diffusion models can match AR-based LLM performance on general language tasks while providing distinct advantages in planning-intensive scenarios and offering unprecedented inference flexibility through arbitrary-order generation and dynamic quality-speed optimization.
We release both Dream-Base and Dream-Instruct to facilitate further research in this promising direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="178.25" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 613.78 178.25" width="613.78"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,178.25) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 155.72 C 0 158.98 2.64 161.62 5.91 161.62 L 607.87 161.62 C 611.14 161.62 613.78 158.98 613.78 155.72 L 613.78 5.91 C 613.78 2.64 611.14 0 607.87 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F5F6F6;" fill="#F5F6F6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 155.72 C 1.97 157.89 3.73 159.66 5.91 159.66 L 607.87 159.66 C 610.05 159.66 611.81 157.89 611.81 155.72 L 611.81 5.91 C 611.81 3.73 610.05 1.97 607.87 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.75 152.96)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.53 L 0 20.76 C 0 23.26 2.03 25.29 4.53 25.29 L 95.28 25.29 C 97.78 25.29 99.81 23.26 99.81 20.76 L 99.81 4.53 C 99.81 2.03 97.78 0 95.28 0 L 4.53 0 C 2.03 0 0 2.03 0 4.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.57 4.53 L 1.57 20.76 C 1.57 22.39 2.9 23.72 4.53 23.72 L 95.28 23.72 C 96.91 23.72 98.24 22.39 98.24 20.76 L 98.24 4.53 C 98.24 2.9 96.91 1.57 95.28 1.57 L 4.53 1.57 C 2.9 1.57 1.57 2.9 1.57 4.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 9.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.17em;font-size:11.5pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="76.13"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Takeaways</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 124.4)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:41.23em;--ltx-fo-height:1.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:7.99em;font-size:10pt;" height="134.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 23.45)" width="570.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p5.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:41.23em;">
<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We release Dream 7B, which consistently outperforms existing diffusion language models by substantial margins across diverse benchmarks.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Dream 7B achieves competitive performance with Qwen 2.5 on standard benchmarks (general language understanding, mathematical reasoning, and code generation) while exhibiting superior planning abilities and novel inference flexibility features that naturally emerge from the diffusion modeling paradigm.</span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Diffusion Models for Text</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models were originally developed for continuous domains like image generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Building on their remarkable success, a number of works have extended diffusion techniques to text generation tasks by modeling text in a continuous embedding space, building continuous diffusion language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dieleman et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Among these approaches, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2023)</a></cite> demonstrated the feasibility of applying diffusion models within a pre-training and fine-tuning framework on a small-scale transformer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To fit the discrete nature of text, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al. (2022)</a></cite> introduced discrete diffusion, which operates on the discrete vocabulary space, establishing the foundation for discrete diffusion methods.
In this formulation, diffusion forward steps progressively with either <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[MASK]</span> tokens (leading to an absorbing state) or random tokens (resulting in a uniform state), and the reverse process reconstructs the original text from these noised sequences. Building on this foundation, several studies have improved the training objectives for discrete diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Besides, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2025c)</a></cite> have shown benefits by initializing discrete diffusion models with pre-trained masked language models (MLMs, e.g., BERT; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. 2019</a></cite>).
Recent studies have highlighted diffusion language models’ advantages for complex reasoning and planning tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. Moreover, combining diffusion and autoregressive methods through block generation has emerged as a promising approach, demonstrating a flexible trade-off between parallelism and sequential coherence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arriola et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Diffusion Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulrajani and Hashimoto (2023a)</a></cite> analyzed the scaling laws for continuous diffusion language models, highlighting that continuous diffusion models require substantially longer training compared to autoregressive (AR) counterparts for optimal computational efficiency. In the discrete domain, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al. (2024)</a></cite> showed that masked diffusion models (MDMs) could achieve perplexities comparable to or exceeding GPT-2-level AR models.
Encouraged by these results, recent efforts have scaled diffusion language models to billions of parameters.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2025a)</a></cite> demonstrated the applicability of 1.1B-scale MDMs in language tasks such as question answering. Instead of training from scratch, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al. (2025)</a></cite> proposed adapting existing AR language models (GPT-2 and LLaMA, from 127M to 7B parameters) into diffusion-based counterparts termed DiffuGPT and DiffuLLaMA. Concurrently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2025b)</a></cite> presented LLaDA, an 8B-parameter diffusion language model trained from scratch, which achieved performance competitive with LLaMA3-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Furthermore, Mercury Coder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inception Labs, 2025</a>)</cite> has recently shown commercial applicability and efficiency in code generation tasks. Overall, our work builds upon the masked diffusion paradigm and introduces new training techniques to push the performance further, highlighting the potential of diffusion-based models as an alternative to autoregressive LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminary</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Auto-regressive Modeling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\coloneq({\bm{x}}^{1},\dots,{\bm{x}}^{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\coloneq({\bm{x}}^{1},\dots,{\bm{x}}^{N})</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from a data distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q({\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. A fundamental approach in sequence modeling decomposes the joint probability of tokens into a product of conditional probabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jelinek, 1980</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al., 2000</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\bm{\theta}}({\mathbf{x}})=p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{x}}^{1})\hskip-14.22636pt\underbrace{\prod_{n=2}^{N}p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{x}}^{n}\mid{\bm{x}}^{1:n-1}),}_{\text{progressive left-context prediction}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>progressive left-context prediction</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\bm{\theta}}({\mathbf{x}})=p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{x}}^{1})\hskip-14.22636pt\underbrace{\prod_{n=2}^{N}p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{x}}^{n}\mid{\bm{x}}^{1:n-1}),}_{\text{progressive left-context prediction}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the model parameters and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{1:n-1}\coloneqq{\bm{x}}^{1},\dots,{\bm{x}}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{1:n-1}\coloneqq{\bm{x}}^{1},\dots,{\bm{x}}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the preceding tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Discrete Diffusion Modeling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Discrete diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021a</a>)</cite> are a class of latent variable models characterized by a forward noising process and a learned reverse denoising process. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the noised sequence at timestep <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[MASK]</span> noise is employed. The forward process <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q({\mathbf{x}}_{1:T}|{\mathbf{x}}_{0})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\mathbf{x}}_{1:T}|{\mathbf{x}}_{0})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> progressively corrupts the original data <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\coloneqq{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\coloneqq{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> into a sequence of increasingly noisy tokens (replaced with <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[MASK]</span> tokens) <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{1:T}\coloneqq{\mathbf{x}}_{1},\dots,{\mathbf{x}}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{1:T}\coloneqq{\mathbf{x}}_{1},\dots,{\mathbf{x}}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The forward process continues until the sequence is fully denoised at <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is partially noised using a noise schedule <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (where for each token the probability of remaining unmasked is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The backward process learns to gradually denoise the masked sequence back to the original data distribution by iteratively predicting masked tokens as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> moves from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. This denoising process can be modeled as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{{\bm{\theta}}}({\mathbf{x}})=\sum_{{\mathbf{x}}_{1:T}\sim q}p({\mathbf{x}}_{T})\hskip-22.76219pt\underbrace{\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{{\bm{\theta}}}({\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t}).}_{\text{progressive full-context prediction}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>progressive full-context prediction</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\bm{\theta}}}({\mathbf{x}})=\sum_{{\mathbf{x}}_{1:T}\sim q}p({\mathbf{x}}_{T})\hskip-22.76219pt\underbrace{\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{{\bm{\theta}}}({\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t}).}_{\text{progressive full-context prediction}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The model parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be optimized by minimizing the negative log-likelihood of the clean data <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The discrete time formulation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,T)</annotation></semantics></math> restricts <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to predetermined noise levels, which can introduce bias. To mitigate this limitation and enable flexible sampling across arbitrary noise levels, we adopt continuous-time parameterization where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> ranges continuously over <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This continuous formulation allows the forward process to be expressed as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq s&lt;t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq s&lt;t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We employ discrete diffusion with absorbing states and simplify the objective as weighted cross-entropy losses:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}),t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1),{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{0})}w(t)\sum_{n=1}^{N}\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}}_{t}^{n}=\texttt{MASK}]}\log p_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{n}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MASK</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}),t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1),{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{0})}w(t)\sum_{n=1}^{N}\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}}_{t}^{n}=\texttt{MASK}]}\log p_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{n}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the indicator function <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}_{t}^{n}}=\texttt{MASK}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MASK</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}_{t}^{n}}=\texttt{MASK}]}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that the loss is computed only on masked token positions, and
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(t)\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t)\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>
is a time-dependent reweighting term determined by a transformation of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For instance, when the forward process adopts the noise schedule <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=1-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=1-t</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding weight becomes <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(t)=\frac{1}{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t)=\frac{1}{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, which assigns greater importance to denoising steps closer to the clean data (i.e., as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>).
As demonstrated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al. (2025)</a></cite>, this weighted cross-entropy objective Equation. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Discrete Diffusion Modeling ‣ 3 Preliminary ‣ Dream 7B: Diffusion Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) represents an effective reformulation of the ELBO, which enables optimization of the mask predictor <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot|{\mathbf{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot|{\mathbf{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by providing a tractable variational upper bound on the negative log-likelihood.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Approach</h2>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2508.15487v1/ar_dream.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/104;" width="476" height="104"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
Comparison of autoregressive modeling and diffusion modeling in Dream. Dream predicts all the masked tokens in a shifted manner, allowing for maximumly architectural alignment and weight initialization with AR models.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>AR-based LLM Initialization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on DiffuLLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we find that leveraging pre-trained weights from existing autoregressive (AR) models provides a non-trivial initialization strategy for diffusion language models.
In Dream, we adopt the "Shift Operation" strategy in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al. (2025)</a></cite> to maintain the inherent shift operation characteristic of AR models during the transition to diffusion-based training.
Specifically, autoregressive models are trained so that the hidden representation at position <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the information necessary to predict the token at position <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather than disrupting this learned positional relationship, our approach preserves this shift mechanism throughout the diffusion training process. The model continues to utilize hidden state <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate predictions for position <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which contrasts with conventional diffusion models that attempt to predict masked tokens at their original positions. This design choice ensures that the diffusion learning process remains aligned with the pretrained representations, preventing the transition from autoregressive to diffusion modeling from disrupting the foundational sequence understanding embedded in the pre-trained weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The AR initialization strategy offers computational advantages by building upon established sequence modeling capabilities rather than learning these representations from scratch, as evidenced by our experimental results in Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Effect of AR Initialization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Dream 7B: Diffusion Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>. Furthermore, this approach maximizes the utility of existing pre-trained AR language models. Once superior AR LLMs become available, our framework enables rapid iteration toward improved diffusion models without requiring costly from-scratch training, effectively leveraging the collective efforts of the research community in autoregressive model development.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2508.15487v1/cart2.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/80;" width="333" height="80"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
Illustration of the context-adaptive token-level noise rescheduling mechanism. Dream re-decides the noise level for each mask token by measuring its context informationness.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Context-Adaptive Token-Level Noise Rescheduling</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard diffusion training for text applies uniform sentence-level timesteps, which ignores the varying contextual dependencies of individual tokens. In conventional discrete diffusion training, a single timestep <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled to determine the overall noise level for the entire sequence, after which the model performs denoising on all the masked positions uniformly. For instance, when adopting the noise schedule <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=1-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=1-t</annotation></semantics></math>, the weighting term <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Discrete Diffusion Modeling ‣ 3 Preliminary ‣ Dream 7B: Diffusion Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) becomes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, since learning occurs at the token level, the actual noise experienced by each token may deviate from the global timestep <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. An example is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 AR-based LLM Initialization ‣ 4 Approach ‣ Dream 7B: Diffusion Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, a shared sentence-level <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned to the three mask tokens in the standard discrete diffusion modeling, while the first token should inherently be associated with a lower noise level than subsequent tokens, as it benefits from richer contextual conditioning. This discrepancy leads to suboptimal learning, particularly for tokens with differing degrees of contextual information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this limitation, we move from sequence-level noise scheduling to token-level noise scheduling. We introduce CART (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ontext-<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>daptive noise <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>escheduling at <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>oken-level), which measures the contextual informativeness for each token individually and dynamically reassigns appropriate noise levels based on the current context. This approach provides fine-grained, token-specific guidance that better reflects the varying difficulty and contextual dependencies across different positions in the sequence.
Specifically, the objective with CART is represented as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}),t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1),{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{0})}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}}_{t}^{n}=\texttt{MASK}]}w(t,{\mathbf{x}}_{t},n)\log p_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{n}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MASK</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}}_{0}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}),t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1),{\mathbf{x}}_{t}\sim q({\mathbf{x}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}}_{0})}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}}_{t}^{n}=\texttt{MASK}]}w(t,{\mathbf{x}}_{t},n)\log p_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{n}|{\mathbf{x}}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(t,{\mathbf{x}}_{t},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t,{\mathbf{x}}_{t},n)</annotation></semantics></math> is a generalized term of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t)</annotation></semantics></math> that can be flexibly designed.
In practice, we employ a mixture of geometric distributions to quantify the information contribution of each clean token relative to the noised tokens,
which is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w(t,{\mathbf{x}}_{t},n)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}}_{t}^{i}\neq\texttt{MASK}]}\operatorname{Geo}(p,|n-i|-1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MASK</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>Geo</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t,{\mathbf{x}}_{t},n)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bm{1}_{[\mathrm{{\mathbf{x}}}_{t}^{i}\neq\texttt{MASK}]}\operatorname{Geo}(p,|n-i|-1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> controls the sharpness of the geometric distribution. A smaller <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> causes clean tokens to tend to contribute uniformly to all masked tokens in the sequence, while a larger <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> forces clean tokens to have a greater influence on the nearby masked tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Training</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Architecture</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dream models are built upon the Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, maintaining full compatibility with existing autoregressive language models while enabling diffusion-based training. Specifically, Dream-7B adopts the same model configuration as Qwen2.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pretraining</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The training objective is to model the data distribution <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. We estimate Equation (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Discrete Diffusion Modeling ‣ 3 Preliminary ‣ Dream 7B: Diffusion Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) via the Monte Carlo method for stochastic gradient descent training. Our training corpus comprises 580 billion tokens spanning text, mathematics, and code domains. The data is sourced from three primary repositories: Dolma v1.7 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soldaini et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for general domain text, OpenCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for programming and code datasets, and DCLM-Baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for curated high-quality text and mathematical content. We exclusively utilize open-source datasets to demonstrate the effectiveness of our paradigm and ensure reproducibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Supervised Fine-Tuning</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SFT training objective is to model the conditional distribution <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(r_{0}|p_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(r_{0}|p_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the response. During fine-tuning, noise is applied only to the response portion of the sequence, keeping the prompt intact to preserve instruction following capabilities. To align Dream with user instructions, we curate a dataset containing 1.8M instruction-response pairs from Tulu 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and SmolLM 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Allal et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Tasks</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated Dream on a comprehensive suite of benchmarks designed to assess its capabilities across multiple domains. For general language understanding, we employed tasks such as MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, BBH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzgun et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, ARC-E <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, ARC-C <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, HellaSwag <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zellers et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, WinoGrande <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakaguchi et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, PIQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bisk et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and RACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2017</a>)</cite> to test knowledge, reasoning, and commonsense abilities. For mathematical and scientific reasoning, we used GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2020</a>)</cite> for numerical computation and problem-solving, and GPQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for scientific understanding. Code generation abilities were evaluated through HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and MBPP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021b</a>)</cite> to measure programming capabilities. The IFEval benchmark specifically measured the model’s capacity to adhere to given instructions for instruction following assessment.
For planning tasks, we evaluate performance on the Countdown and Sudoku tasks from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, and Trip planning task from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, which assess planning capabilities with specific objectives.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span> Comparison of base models on standard evaluation benchmarks. * indicates Dream 7B, LLaDA 8B, Qwen2.5 7B and LLaMA3 8B are evaluated under the same protocol. The best results are bolded and the second best are underlined. Our results are highlighted in <span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E8F0FE;">blue</span>.
</figcaption>
